        {
            "source_entity_instance": "northern EMME",
            "source_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "relation": "HAS_CLIMATE_ZONE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Temperate",
            "target_entity_type": "ClimateZone",
            "paragraph": "The climate of the northern EMME is mostly temperate with warm to hot, dry summers, occasional droughts, and mild, relatively wet winters (Bolle 2003; Lionello et al. 2006).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "southern EMME",
            "source_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "relation": "HAS_CLIMATE_ZONE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Dry",
            "target_entity_type": "ClimateZone",
            "paragraph": "In the southern EMME, an arid hot desert climate prevails, where precipitation is low and vegetation scanty (Issar and Zohar 2007).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "heat waves",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "EMME",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "There is compelling evidence that daytime maximum temperatures (TX) in the EMME are increasing, which is projected to continue in the future, leading to extended heat waves. While heat waves are defined relative to local conditions, it is evident that the level of discomfort is influenced by the already very high summer temperatures. Generally, heat waves are the type of weather extreme with most casualties.",
